  * Shane Mc Cormack agreeing to license his contribution under the
    AGPL, which would settle it outright; or
  * the remaining upstream lines being replaced by work this project
    owns, at which point this section becomes a historical note.

The tracking issue is referenced from the repository's issue list. Until
one of the two lands, a recipient's position with respect to those
specific lines is exactly what it was before this notice existed:
unlicensed, and relied on at the recipient's own assessment. Nothing
here makes it worse, and pretending the question away would have.


Third-party material inside the published image
-----------------------------------------------

The Docker image this repository publishes contains software that this
project neither wrote nor distributes in source form here. The two that
matter to anybody redistributing the image are:

  * **Dell OpenManage Server Administrator** (the `srvadmin-*` packages),
    installed at build time from Dell's own repository at
    <https://linux.dell.com/repo/hardware/>. It is proprietary software,
    governed by the Dell End User License Agreement that accompanies it,
    and it is the reason this image exists at all. This project holds no
    right to relicense it, states none, and grants none: the AGPL above
    covers the scripts in this repository, never the Dell packages the
    image installs. Anyone redistributing the built image is
    redistributing Dell's software and is bound by Dell's terms for it,
    independently of anything this project says.

  * the **base image** and the distribution packages it carries
    (`perl`, `passwd`, `procps-ng`, `kmod` and their dependencies), each
    under its own licence, most of them GPL or permissive.

They are aggregated with this program, not derived from it, and their
terms are the ones shipped inside the image itself.


